course: dictionary
lesson: 12301
title: A formal word for a lot
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grand because they belong in books and reports. Copiously is one of them. Its clue is in its sound: it sits close to «copy» and «copious», which both mean plenty. When you meet a long word like this, ask: what would I say instead?
explain: «It rained copiously all afternoon, and the streets were full of puddles.» The puddles are a clue. A little rain would not leave streets full of water, so copiously must mean a very large amount. Try your meaning in the sentence: «It rained a lot all afternoon» still makes sense.
explain: «She wept copiously at the end of the film.» Here the word tells you how much she cried, not that she cried. Copiously gives the amount. «He took notes copiously during the history talk» works the same way: he wrote a great many notes.
fact: Copiously means in very large amounts, much more than usual.
fact: Copiously is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens, such as how it rained or how someone wept.
fact: Copiously is a formal word, found in books and reports. Everyday words with the same meaning are «a lot», «heavily» and «loads of».
fact: «Copiously» is often used with rain, tears, blood, sweat or notes: «it rained copiously», «she wept copiously», «he took notes copiously».
fact: A quick check: swap in «a lot» and see if the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have the right meaning.
exercise: «It rained copiously all afternoon.» What does copiously mean? | A little, only a drip now and then | *In very large amounts | Quickly and loudly | Gently, like a soft breeze
exercise: «The cut bled copiously, so Mum called the nurse.» Which everyday word could replace copiously? | faintly | not at all | a little | *a lot
exercise: «She wept copiously at the end of the film.» What is being done copiously? | *Crying | Running | Laughing | Sleeping
test: «He took notes copiously during the talk.» What does copiously tell us? | Only one note was taken | He lost all his notes | He never wrote anything | *He wrote a great many notes
test: Which everyday words mean about the same as copiously? | faintly | slowly | *heavily | never
exam: «The rain fell copiously on the roof.» Which word in this sentence tells how the rain fell? | Rain | *Copiously | Roof | The
